  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,{t}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t).\n$$",
